mod ops

// `Drop` lets a type define cleanup that runs automatically when one of its
// values goes out of scope (RAII). The compiler calls `drop` on an owned value
// at the end of its scope (and on early return/break), before freeing the
// value's heap box and recursively dropping its fields. `drop` takes an `mut`
// borrow — it cleans up in place and does not consume `self`, so it is never
// re-entered on the same value.
trait Drop {
    fn drop(mut self);
}

// `Copy` is a marker trait: `impl Copy for T {}` declares that values of `T`
// are duplicated on assignment/argument passing instead of moved. It has no
// methods and no vtable — the compiler treats it specially (it validates that
// `T` has no `Drop` impl and all its fields are `Copy`, and it makes `T`
// usable as a `T: Copy` bound). Scalars and raw pointers are `Copy`
// unconditionally; `String` deliberately omits `impl Copy` because it owns its
// buffer and must drop it.
trait Copy {}
